<Lecture
  chapterSlug="06-string-algos"
  lessonSlug="04-text-stats"
  hook="Six functions — five tiny helpers and one assembler. The shape of every text-processing pipeline."
>
  <h2>The six functions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>char_count(text)</code> — non-whitespace chars</li>
    <li><code>word_count(text)</code> — whitespace-split words</li>
    <li><code>longest_word(words)</code> — first on ties</li>
    <li><code>unique_word_count(words)</code> — distinct (use <code>len(set(words))</code>)</li>
    <li><code>starts_with_count(words, prefix)</code> — filter comprehension</li>
    <li><code>text_stats(text)</code> — assembles dict, calls the helpers</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout tone="warning" title="🔒 no_paste mode is ON">
    <p>Graded homework. Type it. Tutor maxes at L2.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The orchestrator</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`def text_stats(text):
    words = text.split()
    return {
        "chars":         char_count(text),
        "words":         word_count(text),
        "longest":       longest_word(words),
        "unique_words":  unique_word_count(words),
    }`}
  />

  <h2>One-line idioms used in the helpers</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`# unique count
print(len(set(["a", "b", "a"])))                   # 2

# starts-with count
print(len([w for w in ["the", "two", "ten"] if w.startswith("t")]))    # 3

# longest_word (tracking-variable from Ch.3)
def longest_word(words):
    if not words: return ""
    best = words[0]
    for w in words[1:]:
        if len(w) > len(best):
            best = w
    return best

print(longest_word(["cat", "bird", "dog"]))        # "bird"
print(longest_word(["one", "two", "ten"]))         # "one"  (first on ties)`}
  />

